      "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
      "question": "Transformer 1 at MAIN has some dbds in its oil, and I need to know if corrosive sulfur could be causing discharges in the paper. Look at dbds over the available window alongside the latest hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene and acetylene. Classify the fault from those five gases, and check co and co2 for paper involvement. Then tell me whether the picture fits copper sulfide deposition driving 'Partial Discharge in Oil' or 'Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking)', or whether it looks more like plain 'Oil Dielectric Degradation', using dielectric_rigidity and interfacial_v as supporting evidence. Give me a verdict with a confidence level and the two strongest pieces of evidence behind it, and recommend whether we should consider a passivator treatment or an oil change. Base the sulfur judgement on dbds only, not on the gases. If dbds has no data in the window, say that corrosive sulfur can't be assessed, and give the discharge versus degradation verdict from the gases and oil quality alone.",
      "answer": "**Verdict: this does not look like copper sulfide driving 'Partial Discharge in Oil' or 'Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking)', and it does not look like 'Oil Dielectric Degradation' either. The gases point to a high-temperature thermal fault (T3, above 700 \u00b0C). Confidence: high (about 85%).**\n\nThe DGA tool was unavailable (\"LLM unavailable\"), so I worked out the IEC 60599 Rogers ratios myself from the latest reading (2024-01-19).\n\n**Corrosive sulfur (based on dbds only)**\n- dbds had data for the whole window, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19: 5 readings, 18\u201320, mean 19, latest 19. It is steady, not rising.\n- That is well above the roughly 5 mg/kg level usually taken to mean potentially corrosive oil (assuming dbds is reported in mg/kg). So corrosive sulfur is present and copper sulfide deposition is possible.\n\n**Fault from the five gases (ppm)**\nLatest readings: H\u2082 2850, CH\u2084 7400, C\u2082H\u2086 5470, C\u2082H\u2084 16650, C\u2082H\u2082 7.\n\n| Ratio | Value | Code |\n|---|---|---|\n| C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 | 0.0004 | 0 |\n| CH\u2084/H\u2082 | 2.60 | 2 |\n| C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 | 3.04 | 2 |\n\n- The code 0-2-2 means a thermal fault above 700 \u00b0C (T3). A Duval triangle check agrees: about 69% C\u2082H\u2084, 31% CH\u2084, 0.03% C\u2082H\u2082, which falls in T3.\n- Partial discharge would need CH\u2084/H\u2082 below 0.1. Here it is 2.6.\n- Sparking would need C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 above 1. Here it is 0.0004, and acetylene is only 7 ppm.\n\n**Paper involvement**\n- CO is 32 ppm and CO\u2082 is 1340 ppm, so CO\u2082/CO is about 42. CO is low, so the hot spot is probably not heavily attacking the paper. It looks more like a fault in the metal parts (core, joints or leads) with only mild ageing of the paper.\n- That argues against sulfide deposits in the paper causing discharges there.\n\n**Oil quality**\n- Dielectric strength (dielectric_rigidity) is 55, interfacial tension (interfacial_v) is 45 and water is 0. Power factor is about 1.0, which I did not weigh.\n- If these are in the usual units (kV and mN/m), the oil is in good condition, which rules out plain 'Oil Dielectric Degradation'.\n\n**Two strongest pieces of evidence**\n1. The gas ratios (CH\u2084/H\u2082 = 2.6, C\u2082H\u2084/C\u2082H\u2086 = 3.0, C\u2082H\u2082/C\u2082H\u2084 close to 0) are clearly thermal, not discharge. So although the dbds level shows corrosive sulfur, there is no sign in the gases that it is causing discharges yet.\n2. Healthy dielectric strength and interfacial tension (55 and 45), together with dry oil, contradict an oil degradation diagnosis.\n\n**Recommendation**\n- **Oil change:** not justified by oil condition. Only consider it later as a way to remove the dbds, once the fault is fixed.\n- **Passivator:** worth considering because dbds is around 19, but not yet. The metal passivator is used up quickly at hot spots above 700 \u00b0C, so it would not last while the fault is active.\n- **First priority:** find and fix the thermal fault. Check core grounding, joints and leads, and tap-changer contacts. Take repeat DGA samples to track how fast the gases are rising.\n- **Temperature alarms:** the latest reading shows the oil temperature alarm flag at 2 and the trip flag at 1, so treat this as urgent.\n- **After the repair:** passivate and keep monitoring passivator depletion and dbds. If sulfur stays a concern, remove the dbds or replace the oil.",
