          "suggestions": "The iot.sensor_stats call returned a null output in the transcript, yet the agent asserts specific windowed dbds statistics (5 readings, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-19, range 18\u201320, mean 19) and windowed values for dielectric_rigidity/interfacial_v/CO/CO2 that are not evidenced by any tool output; only the latest_reading values (dbds 19, CO 32, CO2 1340, dielectric_rigidity 55, interfacial_v 45) are verifiable. When a stats call returns nothing, the agent should retry or fall back to iot.history rather than report fabricated window statistics. The fmsr.interpret_dga call failed ('LLM unavailable'); the manual Rogers/Duval calculation was a reasonable and transparently stated fallback, and the arithmetic (CH4/H2 = 2.6, C2H4/C2H6 = 3.04, C2H2/C2H4 \u2248 0.0004, Duval ~69% C2H4) is correct and does point to a high-temperature thermal fault. However, the task asked for a verdict among 'Partial Discharge in Oil', 'Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking)' or 'Oil Dielectric Degradation'; the agent should have stated which of the three fits best (or least badly) with its confidence, while noting the T3 thermal signature as the dominant finding, and optionally call fmsr.get_failure_modes to confirm mode names. The confidence level, two pieces of evidence, and passivator/oil-change recommendation were otherwise well presented."
